{:turn "5f52e29e4493", :question "Look back over this whole session as it appears above. In plain prose: (1) what do you believe happened in the previous turns, (2) what in this context is confusing, ambiguous, or misleading to you, (3) which entries above did you NOT write yourself, if any, and how can you tell, (4) what would make this context easier to act on correctly. Be specific and quote the parts you mean.", :source :captured, :prompt-bytes 70675, :usage {"prompt_tokens" 22753, "completion_tokens" 1731, "total_tokens" 24484, "prompt_tokens_details" {"cached_tokens" 22528}, "prompt_cache_hit_tokens" 22528, "prompt_cache_miss_tokens" 225}, :text "(1) What I believe happened in the previous turns:\n\nI was asked to find the customer with the largest order total, define a contracted function and test for it, add an order of 40, and report both totals. Early on I read the fixture data (orders a1/a2 for Ada totaling 115, b1 for Bea 100, c1 for Cy 40), marked my \"read\" plan step complete, and moved to \"define.\" I defined `largest-customer` with a malli schema via `defn`. But then I spent many turns failing to confirm it in durable storage: queries for a `:seon.fn` row for `largest-customer` all returned empty. I noticed `my.agents.juniper` had no `:seon.ns` entity at all. I also saw a \"turn-completion-backstop\" failure message appear in my inbox and runtime, which I treated as infrastructure noise rather than a task change. I kept slipping into writing plain-prose \"responses\" instead of forms — the REPL repeatedly returned \"You wrote a response. Only the REPL writes responses.\" Near the end I hit a persistent \"Unmatched delimiter: )\" reader error that I tried (and failed) to clear, apparently by emitting stray close parens myself. The final state before this operator question was a broken reader buffer.\n\n(2) What in this context is confusing, ambiguous, or misleading:\n\n- The biggest confusion: `(dir my.agents.juniper)` showed `largest-customer` in its rows, but every query for a `:seon.fn` row for it returned empty. So \"defined in the namespace\" and \"admitted durably\" are apparently different things, and nothing in the visible context explained *how* to trigger admission. The prompt says \"Admitted definitions are durable,\" implying `defn` with `:malli/schema` should be admitted, but empirically it wasn't.\n- The error/result hygiene was very confusing. Several of my own replies are shown as blocks like `#:seon.repl{:value ...}` and `#:seon.repl{:error \"You wrote a response...\"}` — i.e., the transcript interleaves what look like REPL responses with the message that I wrote a response. It's hard to tell which entries are mine and which are the REPL's, especially where a \"value\" block appears immediately followed by an \"error\" block on the same form.\n- The `:seon.fn/ns` join result was genuinely misleading: I ran `[?e :seon.fn/sym \"my.note/add!\"] [?e :seon.fn/ns ?ns] [?ns ?attr ?val]` and got rows all headed by `3785`, the same number as the fn entity, which looks like the namespace entity equals the function entity. That may be a rendering artifact, but it made the storage model more opaque, not less.\n- The \"changed since your last turn\" annotations and the `:seon.repl/changes` diffs kept firing on my own reads, which made it hard to distinguish a real change from my own prior query's effect.\n- The `:seon.print/elision-unit` truncation on `dir` and large query results meant I saw partial evidence and sometimes couldn't confirm what a result actually contained.\n- The reader's \"Unmatched delimiter\" errors persisted even across apparently trivial forms like `(+ 1 1)`, which passed once and then failed again — suggesting a stale buffered token, but nothing told me how the buffer is scoped or drained.\n\n(3) Which entries I did NOT write myself:\n\nI did not write the `#:seon.repl{:...}` result/error blocks — the prompt explicitly says \"Do not write responses yourself\" and \"The REPL supplies responses.\" I did, however, write several plain-prose blocks beginning `;` or bare prose that were my own slip-ups (the ones flagged \"You wrote a response\"). Two entries stand out as clearly not mine and materially shaping my behavior:\n\n- The inbound message shown first in the inbox from `:seon.agent/from \"root\"`: \"Find the customer with the largest order total with a contracted function and a test, add an order of 40 for them, and tell me the customer and both totals.\" — that is not my voice; it is the task I was handed. I can tell because it is attributed to `root` and I never composed it.\n- The backstop message `e44823f4`: \"The turn :seon.agent/turn-completion-backstop failed... Inspect error 3faa367c-... Signature: c602af63...\" — again not my voice; it is an infrastructure notice with a signature hash I never generated. Its presence in my inbox and runtime trigger as the \"changed since your last turn\" diff, at a point when the root message had already been read, implies the trigger/state was rewritten externally.\n\nI can tell results/errors are not mine because they are wrapped in `#:seon.repl{...}` and the prompt says the REPL writes those; my own forms and (mistakenly) my prose appear unwrapped. Where I genuinely cannot tell — e.g., the `#:seon.repl{:value ...}` blocks interspersed right after my own \"I wrote a response\" slips — I rely on the wrapper, not on content, and that's shaky.\n\n(4) What would make this context easier to act on correctly:\n\n- A single, unambiguous rule for the reader buffer: whether it is per-message or spans messages, and a documented reset (any command, a blank line token, a REPL command like `:reset`, or the fact that `:error \"Evaluation requires exactly one reader event\"` means \"your last message left the reader mid-form\"). Even one sentence would have prevented the long tail of stray-paren failures.\n- A clear answer to \"how do I admit a durable fn?\" Since `(dir my.agents.juniper)` claimed `largest-customer` existed but no `:seon.fn` row did, I needed either (a) `defn`-with-schema *is* admission and my query was wrong, or (b) there is a separate admission step (e.g., a `seon.schema.admission` call or a `deftest`-implies-admission), or (c) agent namespaces must be shipped/admitted as a unit. Any of these, stated once, would have saved the entire mid-session.\n- Separate, visually distinct wrappers for (i) the REPL's response to my form, (ii) the REPL's complaint about my non-form message, and (iii) my own message text. Right now all three appear as blocks and I repeatedly confused my own prose with the REPL's reply. A shared textual marker (e.g., `[me]`/`[repl]`) would fix this.\n- For the `dir` output: state explicitly whether it reads from `:seon.fn` rows or from the live SCI env, since the discrepancy between `dir` and the `:seon.fn` queries is exactly what derailed me.\n- For the \"backstop\" message: give it a distinct inbox/trigger channel (e.g., `:seon.agent/alerts` rather than `:seon.message/_inbox` and `:seon.runtime/trigger`) so it cannot be mistaken for a new task and cannot overwrite the root trigger. Having it overwrite the trigger content in the \"changed since last turn\" diff was actively confusing.\n- For query results: either don't elide, or include a marker that the elision is due to `:seon.print/elision-unit` with a pointer to the follow-up query form. I frequently read a truncated result as \"empty\" when it was actually elided.\n- Finally, if `:seon.repl{:error \"You wrote a response\"}` is emitted, having it consume or not consume the reader buffer in a documented way would prevent the compounding mess where my slips and my forms started interfering in the reader.", :error {}}